A||N||9||SHalliday||Help! I need headliner replaced! ||||5||
Z||000000||SHalliday||06-18-2000||02:09 PM||||Just recently purchased an 87 GT Fiero. Headlner s shot. The materal s not only saggng but the headliner backing (cardboard, whatever)has multiple cracks and is sagging as well. Anyone else have this problem? <P>Just bought the car and with money a little tight would rather not fork out the cash for a new one. Any suggestions other than purchasing a new liner?<P>Scott Halliday<BR>87 GT Fiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||06-18-2000||03:56 PM||||Hi Scott........I just redid the headliner in my '87GT........you can reinforce the headliner board (provided you remove it in one piece). I put fiberglass cloth and resin on the back side (the side that goes against the top of the car) and then completely fiberglasses the inside (the side that recieves the headliner material). I filled the holes (sunvisor, clothes hanger, etc) with resin and then drilled them out after the resin dried (this made it much stronger).......then, with one square yard of foam backed headliner fabric from the upholstery shop ($7.50) I sprayed three coats of 3M spray adhesive on the headliner board and one coat on the material and placed it on the board. When dried I cut out the sunroof area (leaving 2" all around) and trimmed the front and back (leaving 2") to tuck over. The sides can be trimmed flush with the board as the molding pieces cover the sides.......<P>That's basically it -- takes a little time but it can be done...........here's a pic of the finished installation....... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headliner.jpg"> <P>you will have to remove all of the interior trim panels tho to do the job and it is far easier if you remove the seats too (makes maneuvering much easier) -- be careful on the panels that have the speaker grills on them as there is a black tar substance on the back edges usually - I used goo gone and got that all off before I reinstalled the headliner so I didn't get the black goop on the headliner and window........||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||06-18-2000||03:59 PM||||It costs around $20 to recover it yourself.  I got the foam backed headliner fabric at a fabric store for about $8/yard.  I got 1-1/4 yards so I could also recover the sunvisors.  The same store also had the spray glue for about $10/can.  One brand is 3M Foamfast74.  If you are going to use spray adhesive be sure that it is made for foam backed fabrics.  Regular spray adhesive lets go after about a day.  The correct adhesive sprays on like silly string.  Spray both the fabric back and the headliner board.  You'll have about ten minutes to position the fabric and smooth out the wrinkles before it starts to set up.<P>Some have painted celar varnish ( I think) on the fiber headliner board to make it more durable.  If it has cracks you can repair them using cloth strips as a babdage and the spray adhesive or fiberglass resin.<P>It's not a hard job and you'll like the results.<P>In the meantime you can get upholstry twist pins from Walmart for about 89 cents for a pack of ten.  They look like thumb tacks with corkscrew shaped pins.  Simply screw them up into the headliner to hold the drooping fabric up until you can make the proper repair.  On an old van I was selling I got a bunch of the twist pins and painted them to match the fabric and installed them in a Roll'N'Tuck pattern. Looked pretty good and sold the van.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||06-18-2000||04:40 PM||||I saw Terry's re-cover, and it looks good!!<BR>I also did my 78coupe the exact way. It's one of those jobs that you just have to take your time and BE LIKE NIKE======"Just Do It" it's not hard at all and the price is right.<BR>Be sure to brush off all the old foam junk<BR>left over on the board after you remove the old material.<P>Good Luck!||||reg||14
Z||000004||StuGood||06-19-2000||12:03 AM||||I had success with fiberglassing a headliner board also.  After you apply the resin to the board, though, be sure and let it cure thoroughly and leave it outside for a day or two before you reinstall it in the car.  Otherwise, it'll smell like the resin.   In my case, I left it out in the summer sun for two whole days, to make sure the "resin smell" was all cooked out.       ||||reg||14
Z||000005||Gokart||06-19-2000||06:21 AM||||I just did mine. JC Whitney has the exact match for gray. If anyones interested it's part # 78CR5664NF-med gray foam backed tricot material. Get 2 yards and it'll be enough for 2 replacement jobs.||||reg||14
Z||000006||SHalliday||06-19-2000||08:50 AM||||Thanks for all the helpful suggestions. The actual headliner itself is warped and has broken into a number of different pieces. I am unsure if it is in good enough shape to be fibreglassed back together. If I am unable to find a replacement I guess I will have to give it a try. <P>Thanks again for all the helpful information. <P>Scott Halliday<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||14
Z||000007||tgowens||06-19-2000||02:20 PM||||Scott, you can always check the junk yards - that's where mine came from - cost $5 -- the one I took out of the car in the beginning broke into several pieces when a stack of boxes fell over on it........just take all the panels out of the caar and <I>carefully</I> remove your headliner board -- if it isn't <B>totally</B> demolished it can be mended using the fiberglass cloth and resin.....if you want (i did) you can sand down any rough spots the resin makes after it completely dries........<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||06-19-2000||02:47 PM||||There was someone at Daytona last year and this year selling replacement fiberglass headliner boards.  Maybe some one from those shows has a name associated with the product.  There were the different styles with and without sunroofs.  They weren't free, but the looked real nice for the replacement headliner board.  The original is very flimsy.  And if you get it out in one piece, it can be recovered.  I had a auto upolstry shop do one.  I supplied the fabric, purchased thru Tim Perrego.  The final fit was great.||||reg||5
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-20-2000||10:21 AM||||The Fiero Store has the boards for around $160.  A little steep, IMO.  <A HREF="http://www.leaderind.com" TARGET=_blank>www.leaderind.com</A>  for the Fiero Store.||||reg||1
